<article class="env-lemma" id="0FCS">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FCS">Lemma <span data-tag="0FCS">13.28.5</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{B}$ be a weak Serre subcategory of the abelian category $\mathcal{A}$. There is a canonical isomorphism </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  K_0(\mathcal{B}) \longrightarrow K_0(D^ b_\mathcal {B}(\mathcal{A})),\quad [B] \longmapsto [B[0]]  \]
</div>
  <p> The inverse sends the class $[X]$ of $X$ to the element $\sum (-1)^ i[H^ i(X)]$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We omit the verification that the rule for the inverse gives a well defined map $K_0(D^ b_\mathcal {B}(\mathcal{A})) \to K_0(\mathcal{B})$. It is immediate that the composition $K_0(\mathcal{B}) \to K_0(D^ b_\mathcal {B}(\mathcal{A})) \to K_0(\mathcal{B})$ is the identity. On the other hand, using the distinguished triangles of Remark <a href="/tag/08J5" data-tag="08J5">13.12.4</a> and an induction argument the reader may show that the displayed arrow in the statement of the lemma is surjective (details omitted). The lemma follows. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>